1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:03,500
Their nature on a rampage.

2
00:00:03,501 --> 00:00:07,000
To be in their path is to be in the scariest place on Earth.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:13,000
The size and intensity of hurricanes make them the most feared and destructive of all storms.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:16,000
How do they start? How do we track them?

5
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:18,000
Can we stop them?

6
00:00:18,001 --> 00:00:22,000
We're about to fly into the eye of Hurricane Romo on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Now, Nature Tech Hurricanes on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
A swirling mass of clouds, ferocious winds, lightning, raging seas, torrential floods,

9
00:00:46,720 --> 00:00:49,060
hurricanes are the ultimate in extreme weather.

10
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:59,260
In a typical strong hurricane, the energy budget of that hurricane releases on the order of tens of thousands

11
00:00:59,261 --> 00:01:02,840
of Hiroshima-sized nuclear weapons per second.

12
00:01:03,840 --> 00:01:05,560
Traveling for hundreds of miles

13
00:01:05,561 --> 00:01:09,200
and with potential wind speeds of over 200 miles per hour,

14
00:01:09,820 --> 00:01:12,300
they are capable of bringing nations to their knees.

15
00:01:15,620 --> 00:01:17,480
Those who follow and study them

16
00:01:17,481 --> 00:01:20,640
think of hurricanes almost as living, breathing entities.

17
00:01:22,040 --> 00:01:25,400
Every storm has a different personality and size matters.

18
00:01:25,401 --> 00:01:32,020
We have to know whether a storm is broad and destructive or very small and intense.

19
00:01:32,220 --> 00:01:35,020
They're capable of the virtual destruction of cities.

20
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:44,800
On August 29, 2005, Hurricane Katrina triggered one of the worst natural disasters in the history of the United States.

21
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:50,160
The tragedy left in the wake of this storm makes it more important than ever

22
00:01:50,161 --> 00:01:53,520
that scientists unlock the secrets of these destructive giants.

23
00:01:55,400 --> 00:02:00,020
Some of that learning starts here, with Hunter planes.

24
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:03,820
These flying laboratories travel right through a hurricane

25
00:02:03,821 --> 00:02:07,980
and are equipped to collect information about the storm from the inside out.

26
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,880
October 21st, 2005.

27
00:02:14,780 --> 00:02:20,040
This WC-130J is part of the Air Force Reserve's Weather Reconnaissance Squadron.

28
00:02:20,041 --> 00:02:26,000
It will fly into Hurricane Wilma, a storm that's already registering some record numbers.

29
00:02:27,200 --> 00:02:33,160
The central pressure in Wilma was the lowest central pressure of a hurricane ever measured in the Atlantic Basin.

30
00:02:33,360 --> 00:02:35,120
Quite simply, that's a very intense hurricane.

31
00:02:35,300 --> 00:02:38,960
The lower the pressure, the higher the winds are going to be in the storm.

32
00:02:38,961 --> 00:02:45,920
The plane will fly some 900 miles from Dobbins Air Force Base in Georgia to where Wilma is currently raging,

33
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:49,360
between the Yucatan Peninsula of Mexico and Cuba.

34
00:02:50,040 --> 00:02:56,520
The experienced crew members expect a bumpy ride, with the hurricane's ferocious winds

35
00:02:56,521 --> 00:03:00,660
initiating huge fluctuations in altitude.

36
00:03:00,661 --> 00:03:04,940
The amount of altitude that you actually lose as you penetrate through the hurricane is dependent

37
00:03:04,941 --> 00:03:08,240
upon the severity or how intense the storm is.

38
00:03:08,241 --> 00:03:12,820
But sometimes you can have as much as 2,500 and maybe a 3,000-foot loss in altitude as

39
00:03:12,821 --> 00:03:14,820
you penetrate the eye while the hurricane.

40
00:03:14,821 --> 00:03:17,661
And every flight into every storm is different.

41
00:03:17,662 --> 00:03:20,200
Some are smooth, some are extremely rough.

42
00:03:20,960 --> 00:03:25,600
The plane is racing the clock because scientists at the National Hurricane Center in Miami, Florida,

43
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,240
want its data while the storm is still out at sea.

44
00:03:28,660 --> 00:03:30,980
We're going to catch it right before it goes onshore,

45
00:03:31,480 --> 00:03:35,160
and it's vital that we get our readings still right before the hurricane does get onshore,

46
00:03:35,260 --> 00:03:39,620
because that way they'll know exactly what it's doing as it's landfalling.

47
00:03:39,621 --> 00:03:43,740
The plane will enter the storm at an angle calculated to give it the smoothest ride.

48
00:03:44,820 --> 00:03:50,640
Depending on what the wind speeds are, when we're in the storm, it depends on how we're going to go into it.

49
00:03:50,780 --> 00:03:55,560
The airplane's going to weather vane into the wind, and that's the way we're going to stay on a track, directly to the center of the storm.

50
00:03:56,360 --> 00:04:01,120
If we just went straight towards the eye of the storm without grabbing into the wind as we were calling,

51
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:06,140
we would just pretty much be blown around the storm by the winds,

52
00:04:06,520 --> 00:04:09,420
especially with 150 mile an hour winds is what they're calling right now.

53
00:04:09,940 --> 00:04:14,420
Their main target is the eye of the storm, where the most important readings must be taken.

54
00:04:14,820 --> 00:04:18,820
We want to go to the IKID, we want to mark not only movement but pressure.

55
00:04:18,821 --> 00:04:21,820
Because the pressure is dropping, then they know it's intensifying.

56
00:04:21,821 --> 00:04:25,820
If the pressure is coming back up, then they know it's probably weakening.

57
00:04:25,821 --> 00:04:29,820
A flight meteorologist is in charge of collecting the data.

58
00:04:29,821 --> 00:04:34,820
This particular console collects the horizontal data as we fly along.

59
00:04:34,821 --> 00:04:40,820
That is, we're measuring temperature, pressure, humidity, wind speed, and direction at a very rapid rate.

60
00:04:40,821 --> 00:04:43,820
Typically one sample per second.

61
00:04:44,820 --> 00:04:50,920
Besides collecting information from instruments on board the plane, the crew also utilizes

62
00:04:50,921 --> 00:04:58,000
devices called drop sondes, which are released beneath the plane and fall by parachute down

63
00:04:58,001 --> 00:04:59,000
into the sea.

64
00:04:59,001 --> 00:05:07,060
The drop sond is a cylinder 3 inches in diameter by 18 inches long, weighs just over a pound.

65
00:05:07,061 --> 00:05:11,200
It falls at 2,500 feet per minute.

66
00:05:11,201 --> 00:05:15,760
back pressure, temperature, relative humidity and winds twice per second from flight level

67
00:05:15,761 --> 00:05:16,760
down to surface.

68
00:05:16,761 --> 00:05:21,440
From this we get the pressure on the center of the storm, the winds all the way down to

69
00:05:21,441 --> 00:05:24,880
the surface for the National Hurricane Center to improve the forecast.

70
00:05:41,200 --> 00:05:45,420
With the data collected, they plot a course to return to the United States.

71
00:05:46,440 --> 00:05:51,080
They have crisscrossed the storm six times without incident or major turbulence.

72
00:05:52,940 --> 00:05:54,380
The flight ends safely.

73
00:05:55,340 --> 00:05:58,140
For the crew, this mission is more than just a job.

74
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:02,960
Living with, studying, and even having their own families hit by hurricanes,

75
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:07,540
they know firsthand the destruction that the giant storms can cause.

76
00:06:08,360 --> 00:06:12,320
Obviously, most of us were affected by Katrina and have been affected by other storms.

77
00:06:12,880 --> 00:06:18,620
And to a person in the whole unit, we cannot stand to see the landfalling hurricane, the devastation it causes.

78
00:06:21,980 --> 00:06:28,300
The idea for flying airplanes into hurricanes to collect meteorological data was born during World War II.

79
00:06:28,480 --> 00:06:36,300
The person who's given credit, and rightly so, for being the first person to deliberately fly into a hurricane was Colonel Joseph Duckworth.

80
00:06:37,540 --> 00:06:42,180
Duckworth invented a system of training pilots how to fly when the clouds are all around you.

81
00:06:42,460 --> 00:06:46,900
And one day in 1943, a hurricane was approaching the Texas coast.

82
00:06:47,420 --> 00:06:53,620
And Duckworth took another pilot with him and they flew into a hurricane, flew around for a while, flew back, landed safely.

83
00:06:54,220 --> 00:06:57,520
The base weather officer heard about that and he said, hey, I want to go too.

84
00:06:58,140 --> 00:07:01,020
They did it again. This time they took some measurements.

85
00:07:01,021 --> 00:07:10,529
Weather Bureau heard about some of the measurements they had taken, this prompted them to say, you know, we should really be using airplanes to fly reconnaissance in

86
00:07:10,530 --> 00:07:12,200
When the U.S. the hurricanes.

87
00:07:13,680 --> 00:07:20,880
In the six decades since Duckworth's first flight, every effort has been made to make this hazardous duty as safe as possible.

88
00:07:20,881 --> 00:07:29,940
Today, meteorologists are even using an unmanned aircraft called the Aerosonde to fly into

89
00:07:29,941 --> 00:07:33,520
areas of hurricanes that are too treacherous for traditional planes.

90
00:07:33,521 --> 00:07:40,200
The Aerosonde is about six feet long with a wingspan of also about six feet and it has

91
00:07:40,201 --> 00:07:47,720
a small gasoline powered engine which is able to operate for up to 24 hours per flight.

92
00:07:47,721 --> 00:07:53,900
The aerosonde is launched via truck, which rolls down the runway at a fixed speed of 55 miles an hour.

93
00:07:54,300 --> 00:07:56,240
It's a remote-controlled aircraft.

94
00:07:57,180 --> 00:08:03,080
It is steered through commands via GPS tracking, and its location is relayed back to the pilot.

95
00:08:04,040 --> 00:08:08,280
Although it will never replace traditional chaser planes that can carry many more instruments,

96
00:08:09,320 --> 00:08:13,500
the aerosonde is vital for getting right down to the surface of the ocean to make readings.

97
00:08:13,501 --> 00:08:18,500
This is an area that a larger plane can't fly to safely.

98
00:08:18,501 --> 00:08:26,500
The area close to the water in hurricanes is important to measure because much of the

99
00:08:26,501 --> 00:08:32,500
energy exchange between hurricanes and the underlying sea surface occurs in these extremely

100
00:08:32,501 --> 00:08:36,500
high wind conditions near the eyewall of storms.

101
00:08:36,501 --> 00:08:40,500
These monstrous windstorms are known by different names throughout the world.

102
00:08:40,501 --> 00:08:44,000
In the Northwest Pacific Ocean, they're called typhoons.

103
00:08:44,840 --> 00:08:49,140
In Australia and the Indian Ocean, they're known as tropical cyclones.

104
00:08:49,860 --> 00:08:58,180
In the North Atlantic Ocean, the Caribbean Sea, the Gulf of Mexico, and the Northeast Pacific Ocean, they're called hurricanes.

105
00:09:01,400 --> 00:09:03,640
But just what is a hurricane?

106
00:09:03,641 --> 00:09:10,880
The Atlantic hurricane season starts when summer arrives, and the water off the West

107
00:09:10,881 --> 00:09:12,900
African coast begins to warm up.

108
00:09:12,901 --> 00:09:18,480
The important thing about hurricanes is they live on the heat energy stored in the ocean.

109
00:09:18,481 --> 00:09:24,280
All summer the sun shines on the tropical sea, and the water gets to be as warm as bathwater,

110
00:09:24,281 --> 00:09:25,280
basically.

111
00:09:25,281 --> 00:09:30,600
When the hurricane starts, the wind blowing over the water causes evaporation.

112
00:09:30,601 --> 00:09:37,240
The water vapor goes from the sea into the atmosphere, taking the heat that it needs to evaporate from the ocean.

113
00:09:38,040 --> 00:09:41,880
As the column of moist, warm air rises, condensation occurs.

114
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:48,700
This is the process by which water vapor turns into droplets of water, and heat is released.

115
00:09:48,840 --> 00:09:55,700
The heat warms the air column, and the water drops create clouds, which develop into rain and thunderstorms.

116
00:09:55,701 --> 00:10:01,700
These thunderstorms are the basic building blocks of hurricanes.

117
00:10:01,701 --> 00:10:09,380
The storm system begins to operate as a huge heat engine, pulling in warm, humid air near

118
00:10:09,381 --> 00:10:15,960
the ocean's surface and spitting out cooler, drier air into the upper atmosphere.

119
00:10:15,961 --> 00:10:21,140
The system's strong winds are caused by air rushing in from below to replace the air rising

120
00:10:21,141 --> 00:10:22,140
in the storm.

121
00:10:22,141 --> 00:10:24,900
It's kind of like a chain reaction.

122
00:10:24,901 --> 00:10:28,940
You get more and more air rising, more humid air coming in to replace it.

123
00:10:28,941 --> 00:10:33,260
Interaction between the motion of the air and the rotation of the earth causes the storm

124
00:10:33,261 --> 00:10:34,760
to spin.

125
00:10:34,761 --> 00:10:38,100
This is known as the Coriolis effect.

126
00:10:38,101 --> 00:10:41,900
In the northern hemisphere, hurricanes spin in a counterclockwise direction.

127
00:10:41,901 --> 00:10:45,020
In the southern hemisphere, they spin clockwise.

128
00:10:45,021 --> 00:10:49,640
A column of calm air develops in the center of the system.

129
00:10:49,641 --> 00:10:51,640
This is called the eye.

130
00:10:51,641 --> 00:10:59,100
It's a nearly cloudless space, filled with light breezes and clear skies.

131
00:10:59,101 --> 00:11:03,780
The system's raging winds spiral around the eye.

132
00:11:03,781 --> 00:11:08,960
When these winds reach a speed of 74 miles per hour, the storm is officially called a

133
00:11:08,961 --> 00:11:10,860
hurricane.

134
00:11:10,861 --> 00:11:16,460
Trade winds push the entire system west across the Atlantic.

135
00:11:16,461 --> 00:11:22,380
These are rated in categories from 1 to 5, a category 1 being the weakest and a category

136
00:11:22,381 --> 00:11:32,280
5 the strongest, with wind velocities of over 156 miles per hour.

137
00:11:32,281 --> 00:11:37,060
The storms have been the scourge of mankind since before recorded history.

138
00:11:37,061 --> 00:11:42,920
Early native tribes of the Caribbean considered them evil spirits from the sea.

139
00:11:42,921 --> 00:11:45,160
the Mayan storm god was called Urican.

140
00:11:45,161 --> 00:11:50,060
And the storm god of native Caribbean islanders was called Urican.

141
00:11:50,061 --> 00:11:55,420
It is believed that the word hurricane derives from these sources.

142
00:11:55,421 --> 00:12:00,980
Columbus was really the first European to run into a hurricane.

143
00:12:00,981 --> 00:12:03,440
It was on his second voice in the New World.

144
00:12:03,441 --> 00:12:04,960
He actually encountered a storm.

145
00:12:04,961 --> 00:12:08,300
Apparently it wasn't too bad because he survived.

146
00:12:08,301 --> 00:12:12,760
Although weather records are scarce, it appears that storms hit for centuries with about the

147
00:12:12,761 --> 00:12:15,760
same regularity as they do today.

148
00:12:17,760 --> 00:12:21,760
But scientific knowledge of their origins and makeup was non-existent.

149
00:12:21,761 --> 00:12:28,760
It's hard for us to imagine, but by the early 19th century, people didn't really realize

150
00:12:28,761 --> 00:12:30,593
that storms were huge whirlwinds.

151
00:12:30,594 --> 00:12:33,760
The picture they had was kind of like, you know, the wind

152
00:12:33,761 --> 00:12:35,760
came up, it blew, then it stopped.

153
00:12:35,761 --> 00:12:41,760
In the 1820s, self-taught scientist William Redfield became one of the first to start unlocking

154
00:12:41,761 --> 00:12:44,680
blocking the secrets of hurricanes.

155
00:12:44,681 --> 00:12:48,380
He had noticed on a walking trip across Connecticut,

156
00:12:48,381 --> 00:12:51,720
after a big hurricane hit, that trees in one part of the area,

157
00:12:51,721 --> 00:12:54,380
they were all blown down facing the northeast.

158
00:12:54,381 --> 00:12:56,920
A few miles away, all the trees were blown down

159
00:12:56,921 --> 00:12:58,680
facing the southwest.

160
00:12:58,681 --> 00:13:01,000
And he started thinking about this.

161
00:13:01,001 --> 00:13:04,280
You know, what in the world can make a storm blow down

162
00:13:04,281 --> 00:13:07,880
trees this way in one area, and not that far away

163
00:13:07,881 --> 00:13:09,260
facing the other way?

164
00:13:09,261 --> 00:13:15,440
And what he concluded was that large storms like hurricanes were really whirlwinds.

165
00:13:15,441 --> 00:13:19,100
The winds were going around a center.

166
00:13:19,101 --> 00:13:24,400
The hurricane that hit Galveston, Texas on September 8, 1900,

167
00:13:24,401 --> 00:13:28,260
created one of the greatest natural disasters in American history,

168
00:13:28,261 --> 00:13:32,140
killing between 6,000 and 8,000 people.

169
00:13:32,141 --> 00:13:36,440
The people who died in Galveston almost universally drowned.

170
00:13:36,441 --> 00:13:39,940
The waves from the storm destroyed the first line of houses.

171
00:13:40,140 --> 00:13:43,840
The debris from the first line of houses destroyed the second line of houses

172
00:13:43,841 --> 00:13:47,260
and it swept about a third or almost halfway across the city.

173
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:52,780
During the first part of the 20th century, advances in radio communications,

174
00:13:53,280 --> 00:13:54,700
especially from ships at sea,

175
00:13:55,360 --> 00:13:58,460
improved our ability to signal early warnings about the storms.

176
00:13:59,660 --> 00:14:04,240
World War II gave birth to some major technological advances for studying hurricanes.

177
00:14:05,220 --> 00:14:09,920
Besides the development of hurricane hunter planes, it also introduced radar.

178
00:14:11,040 --> 00:14:14,700
The whole idea was to spot enemy airplanes or enemy ships.

179
00:14:15,200 --> 00:14:21,440
When people first started experimenting with radar, they would see fuzzy images and things really caused by the weather.

180
00:14:21,940 --> 00:14:29,700
Radar sends out a radio wave which scatters or reflects off of things, such as an airplane, but also raindrops or ice and clouds.

181
00:14:29,701 --> 00:14:38,920
And it showed that a hurricane is made of spiral bands, you know, bands of thunderstorms that are spiraling in to the eyewall.

182
00:14:39,160 --> 00:14:44,940
And before that radar picture, no one really realized that hurricanes had this structure.

183
00:14:45,780 --> 00:14:48,400
Today's meteorologists use Doppler radar.

184
00:14:49,320 --> 00:14:53,400
Nowadays, anyone who watches television weather is going to hear about Doppler radar.

185
00:14:53,401 --> 00:14:58,400
When a radar sends a beam out to bounce off of a raindrop, say,

186
00:14:58,401 --> 00:15:02,400
the wind is blowing the raindrop away from the radar.

187
00:15:02,401 --> 00:15:05,400
That means a wave coming back is going to have a longer frequency.

188
00:15:05,401 --> 00:15:09,400
You can think of it in a way as it's stretching the wave out.

189
00:15:09,401 --> 00:15:12,400
If the raindrop is going toward the radar,

190
00:15:12,401 --> 00:15:16,400
it's going to squeeze the frequency and give it a higher frequency.

191
00:15:17,400 --> 00:15:19,400
What Doppler radar does, basically,

192
00:15:19,401 --> 00:15:22,400
it's the computer processing of the radar data.

193
00:15:22,401 --> 00:15:28,736
data. Many radar antennas are housed in domes to protect them from the weather.

194
00:15:28,737 --> 00:15:30,100
Doppler radar can

195
00:15:30,101 --> 00:15:35,036
present meteorologists with a giant color scheme of the storm.

196
00:15:35,037 --> 00:15:38,220
The light blues represent precipitation,

197
00:15:38,221 --> 00:15:39,836
say for instance drizzle.

198
00:15:39,837 --> 00:15:44,620
Then when you get into the greens, you start getting into heavier rainfall

199
00:15:44,621 --> 00:15:45,321
amounts.

200
00:15:45,322 --> 00:15:49,440
Then when you get into the yellows and the oranges and the reds, that's very, very intense

201
00:15:49,441 --> 00:15:53,140
precipitation, and that is typically where the strongest winds are located.

202
00:15:53,660 --> 00:15:56,180
Radar has unlocked many hurricane secrets.

203
00:15:56,660 --> 00:16:02,440
But in order to get even more accurate readings, some operators are driving Dopplers on wheels

