207
00:16:19,440 --> 00:16:24,600
Nature Tech Hurricanes will return on Modern Marvels.

208
00:16:25,780 --> 00:16:30,340
We now return to Nature Tech Hurricanes on Modern Marvels.

209
00:16:31,400 --> 00:16:37,260
Although radar is a technological miracle that has given us a detailed look at the inner workings of hurricanes,

210
00:16:38,160 --> 00:16:40,120
it still has some drawbacks.

211
00:16:40,920 --> 00:16:45,920
The farther an object is from a radar, the poorer you are able to see that object.

212
00:16:45,921 --> 00:16:51,340
And that's due to both the spreading of the radar beam and the curving of the Earth away from the radar beam.

213
00:16:51,600 --> 00:16:58,340
If an object like a hurricane eye is far away from a radar, you can't see details in it because it's too far away and it's too blurry.

214
00:16:58,700 --> 00:17:06,100
And you also can't see some of the most important details, those happening near the ground, because the ground is literally over the horizon from the radar.

215
00:17:06,860 --> 00:17:08,740
Are we going to stay good or want me to do a U-turn?

216
00:17:08,900 --> 00:17:09,600
I don't know.

217
00:17:09,601 --> 00:17:10,500
It's getting stronger.

218
00:17:10,501 --> 00:17:16,380
In order to remedy this problem, some intrepid meteorologists are turning to Dopplers on wheels.

219
00:17:18,880 --> 00:17:22,500
The Doppler on wheels are able to drive up into the hurricane,

220
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:27,300
so we can see very, very fine details that are just impossible to see from farther away.

221
00:17:27,720 --> 00:17:33,620
We are 100 times closer than a typical land-based radar, and we sample much, much faster.

222
00:17:33,880 --> 00:17:37,940
We're getting 10,000 to 100,000 data points where a conventional radar might just get one.

223
00:17:37,941 --> 00:17:44,940
Scientists are hoping the device will unlock some secrets of hurricane behavior that so far have eluded them.

224
00:17:44,941 --> 00:17:52,940
The prime focus of the Doppler and Wheels is to study the variability of the winds in the hurricanes as they come onshore.

225
00:17:52,941 --> 00:17:55,940
We know that some neighborhoods experience much more damage than others.

226
00:17:55,941 --> 00:17:59,940
And we also have discovered that there is a lot of variability in the wind field.

227
00:17:59,941 --> 00:18:01,940
Some areas are getting much stronger winds than others.

228
00:18:01,941 --> 00:18:06,940
And what we want to understand is how those winds are related to damage.

229
00:18:06,941 --> 00:18:07,880
That's an important gap.

230
00:18:08,360 --> 00:18:12,080
Is it 150 mile an hour winds for a few seconds, which are the most damaging?

231
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:14,740
Or is it the prolonged effect of 100 mile an hour winds?

232
00:18:15,120 --> 00:18:19,140
It's a dangerous task, but operators take every precaution when deploying the vehicle.

233
00:18:19,720 --> 00:18:26,960
We do detailed surveys of our deployment locations so we know that we're not going to be downwind of sources of debris.

234
00:18:27,140 --> 00:18:28,460
We don't park near big trees.

235
00:18:28,860 --> 00:18:31,160
We don't park in a low area that's going to get flooded.

236
00:18:31,520 --> 00:18:34,700
Even though we're right in the middle of the hurricane, in the worst part of the hurricane,

237
00:18:34,701 --> 00:18:37,900
And we have a perfect safety record because we take these extreme precautions.

238
00:18:38,840 --> 00:18:42,820
Although radar has been an essential tool for hurricane research since World War II,

239
00:18:44,040 --> 00:18:48,560
another major advance came with the introduction of weather satellites during the 1960s.

240
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:53,780
For the first time, we were able to see the awesome scale of storms in one picture.

241
00:18:54,240 --> 00:18:56,480
It really does give you the big picture.

242
00:18:57,080 --> 00:18:59,500
Where are the fronts? Where are the high-pressure centers?

243
00:18:59,501 --> 00:19:00,960
Where are the low-pressure centers?

244
00:19:01,400 --> 00:19:02,720
Where is the hurricane moving?

245
00:19:04,700 --> 00:19:10,300
Satellites provide far more information about hurricanes than simply photographs.

246
00:19:10,301 --> 00:19:14,500
We can see them speed up and slow down.

247
00:19:14,501 --> 00:19:17,180
We can check out the temperatures of their cloud tops.

248
00:19:17,181 --> 00:19:21,960
We can actually keep track of the sea surface temperatures from satellites.

249
00:19:21,961 --> 00:19:30,380
America's first weather satellite, the TIROS-1, was launched in April 1960.

250
00:19:30,381 --> 00:19:34,300
Satellites have given us the ability to have an early warning system for hurricane threats.

251
00:19:34,301 --> 00:19:36,000
to go out more towards the new can.

252
00:19:36,001 --> 00:19:38,880
And thus, to save hundreds of thousands of lives.

253
00:19:42,560 --> 00:19:45,960
In the old days, if the hurricane forecasters were lucky,

254
00:19:46,380 --> 00:19:48,680
a ship might stumble into a breeding storm

255
00:19:48,681 --> 00:19:51,160
and ships would send weather information back.

256
00:19:51,700 --> 00:19:54,420
But hurricanes tend to form over parts of the ocean

257
00:19:54,421 --> 00:19:55,720
where there aren't too many ships.

258
00:19:56,020 --> 00:19:59,440
So the first thing that satellites do is they make sure

259
00:19:59,441 --> 00:20:02,860
that a hurricane is never going to sneak up on any part of the world.

260
00:20:02,861 --> 00:20:11,040
The ability of satellites to show scientists whether a storm is growing or diminishing over a period of time is extremely important for weather prediction.

261
00:20:12,320 --> 00:20:16,660
As we see in this Hurricane Katrina animation loop, it starts out with a very small eye,

262
00:20:17,480 --> 00:20:22,220
and then three days later evolves into a much larger hurricane circulation.

263
00:20:22,740 --> 00:20:28,920
Now we're talking about a wind field that's more than three times the size as it was when it passed close to Key West, Florida,

264
00:20:29,340 --> 00:20:32,520
compared to the time that it made landfall over southeast Louisiana and Mississippi.

265
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:39,860
Besides satellites and radar, the other major tool in hurricane research is the computer.

266
00:20:39,861 --> 00:20:46,860
Computers have been responsible for really some of the big improvements we've seen and forecasts of where a hurricane is going to be.

267
00:20:46,861 --> 00:20:56,340
For instance, in 1990, the National Hurricane Center forecast where a hurricane would be 24 hours from now was on the average about 128 miles from where it actually

268
00:20:56,341 --> 00:20:57,041
ended up.

269
00:20:57,042 --> 00:21:01,860
By 2004, that had been cut pretty much in half down to 70 mile error.

270
00:21:01,861 --> 00:21:09,860
Billions of pieces of weather data are collected by organizations like the National Center for Atmospheric Research in Boulder, Colorado.

271
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:20,860
And processed through supercomputers to create models that show scientists what the weather is doing and what it's likely to do.

272
00:21:21,860 --> 00:21:25,172
The floor there is about 13,000 square feet.

273
00:21:25,173 --> 00:21:28,860
The computer we have right now is an IBM Power 4.

274
00:21:28,861 --> 00:21:29,634
IBM Power 4.

275
00:21:29,635 --> 00:21:34,860
It's one of the faster machines anywhere that does forecasting in near real time.

276
00:21:35,860 --> 00:21:38,860
The supercomputers devour data from every source available.

277
00:21:39,860 --> 00:21:47,860
These are the observations from satellites and from radiations, weather balloons, and aircraft, and buoys and ships on the ocean surface.

278
00:21:48,860 --> 00:21:52,860
All of these go in every time, whether there's a hurricane or not.

279
00:21:52,861 --> 00:22:03,140
When there's a tropical cyclone, we send out special aircraft with instruments that are dropped into hurricanes or remotely sense the hurricanes.

280
00:22:03,500 --> 00:22:10,840
And these can then be assimilated into our models in addition to all of the other data that routinely goes in.

281
00:22:11,320 --> 00:22:18,720
After the information is processed, the resulting computer models are sent to Miami's National Hurricane Center to be evaluated.

282
00:22:18,721 --> 00:22:24,720
We are the final word in terms of the U.S. government.

283
00:22:24,721 --> 00:22:27,246
We're responsible for providing the U.S.

284
00:22:27,247 --> 00:22:30,720
citizens with tropical cyclone forecasts for all storms

285
00:22:30,721 --> 00:22:34,720
throughout the Atlantic and the eastern North Pacific Ocean basins.

286
00:22:34,721 --> 00:22:40,720
One major challenge facing the Hurricane Center is that the computer models of a particular storm

287
00:22:40,721 --> 00:22:43,720
are sent to them by weather forecasters from all over the world.

288
00:22:43,721 --> 00:22:48,720
world. And each interprets the numbers a different way.

289
00:22:48,721 --> 00:22:53,040
Say the UK met the one by the United Kingdom meteorological office to say the storm is going

290
00:22:53,041 --> 00:22:59,040
to go here. No gaps, which is a US Navy global model, says it's going to go there.

291
00:22:59,041 --> 00:23:07,040
Forecasting hurricanes is easy, but validating the models, verifying the forecasts is hard.

292
00:23:07,041 --> 00:23:12,220
Often the Hurricane Center looks at as many as 25 computer models when trying to determine

293
00:23:12,221 --> 00:23:14,980
the course a hurricane will take.

294
00:23:14,981 --> 00:23:19,800
You can go back and look at just about every single storm and you will see that of the

295
00:23:19,801 --> 00:23:24,540
25 or so models, there's always one that seems to do better than the rest of them, but it

296
00:23:24,541 --> 00:23:27,960
isn't always the best one in every particular situation.

297
00:23:27,961 --> 00:23:32,480
We have four computer models that tend to outperform all the other models, and those

298
00:23:32,481 --> 00:23:34,280
are the ones that we tend to rely on the most.

299
00:23:34,281 --> 00:23:38,540
However, we never key our forecast on any one particular model.

300
00:23:38,541 --> 00:23:43,940
We try to look at a consensus to see if the computer models are headed in the same direction.

301
00:23:44,900 --> 00:23:48,740
This consensus approach works well about 85% of the time.

302
00:23:49,160 --> 00:23:53,040
But the rest of the time, someone must step in and make a crucial decision.

303
00:23:54,500 --> 00:23:58,020
One individual, because of their experience, may have seen a certain situation.

304
00:23:58,160 --> 00:24:00,860
They say, well, the models are deficient in this area,

305
00:24:00,861 --> 00:24:02,880
and this is why I think the track should go this way,

306
00:24:02,960 --> 00:24:05,540
or the intensity should be increased or decreased.

307
00:24:05,541 --> 00:24:14,560
The human factor came into play on October 17, 2005, when the Hurricane Center had to predict the course of the super hurricane, Wilma.

308
00:24:16,620 --> 00:24:23,620
Meteorologist Stacy Stewart overrode the computer models and came up with a more accurate reading of the hurricane's potential path.

309
00:24:24,500 --> 00:24:27,860
This was the forecast track that was issued.

310
00:24:27,861 --> 00:24:36,780
There was my day five position, and my position was about 240 nautical miles to the northeast of our most reliable forecast tool that we have.

311
00:24:37,120 --> 00:24:41,340
Five days after this was issued, this is where the hurricane actually ended up being.

312
00:24:41,520 --> 00:24:43,140
So the forecast track was on.

313
00:24:43,540 --> 00:24:50,760
My forecast speed was a little bit fast, but it was still better than our most reliable forecast model.

314
00:24:51,400 --> 00:24:56,980
We may have become more accurate in predicting hurricane behavior, which helps forewarn residents to flee.

315
00:24:57,860 --> 00:25:00,160
But we certainly can't control them.

316
00:25:00,840 --> 00:25:03,840
Wind damage caused by a giant hurricane can be catastrophic.

317
00:25:04,460 --> 00:25:07,020
Yet ironically, it's not the storm's main weapon.

318
00:25:08,100 --> 00:25:10,860
Storm surge is its real knockout punch.

319
00:25:13,280 --> 00:25:19,040
The National Hurricane Center in Miami, Florida, is built to withstand a Category 5 hurricane.

320
00:25:20,060 --> 00:25:22,800
Its reinforced concrete walls are 10 inches thick.

321
00:25:23,400 --> 00:25:27,200
And its backup power system can keep the center running for 10 days.

322
00:25:27,860 --> 00:25:32,180
Nature Tech Hurricanes will return on Modern Marvels.

323
00:25:33,340 --> 00:25:38,000
We now return to Nature Tech Hurricanes on Modern Marvels.

324
00:25:38,660 --> 00:25:42,900
Of all the hurricane's weapons, the most feared is storm surge.

325
00:25:43,680 --> 00:25:48,300
As a hurricane is moving across the ocean, the winds are pushing the water ahead of it.

326
00:25:48,720 --> 00:25:53,900
When the hurricane is out in deep water, a lot of that water flows away underneath the storm.

327
00:25:53,901 --> 00:26:01,720
But as you push the water up on a very shallow coast, there's no place for it to go, so it builds up, comes ashore as a storm surge.

328
00:26:02,420 --> 00:26:07,420
Storm surge is so deadly because many population centers are located along coastlines.

329
00:26:09,100 --> 00:26:15,260
In 1970, a storm surge in East Pakistan killed more than 250,000 people.

330
00:26:16,560 --> 00:26:21,500
And it was storm surge in the 1900 Galveston hurricane that leveled that city.

331
00:26:21,501 --> 00:26:27,740
Scientists have developed computer programs called SLOSH models that help them predict

332
00:26:27,741 --> 00:26:29,380
potential storm surge damage.

333
00:26:31,100 --> 00:26:36,020
SLOSH is an acronym for Sea, Land, and Overland Surges from Hurricanes.

334
00:26:37,860 --> 00:26:43,420
When the storm is actually approaching, you need to know what this storm is likely to do.

335
00:26:43,940 --> 00:26:49,240
For instance, how early in the game is it likely to flood a coastal road that people

336
00:26:49,241 --> 00:26:50,240
might need to get out on?

337
00:26:50,241 --> 00:26:57,360
The information it uses is the shape of the bottom of the ocean at the place where the storm is coming ashore,

338
00:26:57,880 --> 00:27:02,680
the shape of the land, how strong the hurricane is, and the distribution of its wind.

339
00:27:02,681 --> 00:27:08,580
This is SLOSH storm surge forecast model output for the southwest coast of Florida

340
00:27:08,581 --> 00:27:15,100
as Wilma was coming in from the southwest in October of 2005.

341
00:27:15,101 --> 00:27:24,049
What people need to understand from this is that these colors represent the height that the ocean surface is forecast to be above sea level from where it normally is

342
00:27:24,050 --> 00:27:24,750
going to be.

343
00:27:25,300 --> 00:27:30,620
In many of these areas right here, the storm surge forecast is 17 feet.

344
00:27:31,640 --> 00:27:36,260
If your house is only 12 feet above sea level, that means you're going to have 5 feet of water in your house.

345
00:27:36,740 --> 00:27:39,240
If you can't swim, that means you're probably going to drown.

346
00:27:39,241 --> 00:27:45,280
So these kind of models are used both to help emergency managers make their evacuation plans,

347
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:50,280
and also when the storm is actually occurring, where the worst surge might be,

348
00:27:50,480 --> 00:27:54,400
where you might find victims, you know, say on the roofs of buildings.

349
00:27:55,200 --> 00:27:58,080
Storm surge may pack a hurricane's deadliest punch.

350
00:27:58,640 --> 00:28:01,720
But when most people think of hurricanes, they think of wind.

351
00:28:03,980 --> 00:28:08,420
Conventional buildings have a poor record of survival when battered by hurricane-force winds.

352
00:28:09,240 --> 00:28:16,940
Hit by intense gusts, roofs blow off, windows shatter, doors cave in, and entire buildings can be destroyed.

353
00:28:18,740 --> 00:28:23,360
Today, some builders are turning to innovative alternative designs when constructing buildings.

354
00:28:23,920 --> 00:28:25,414
Take the dome, for example.

355
00:28:25,415 --> 00:28:29,120
It's an old idea that's gaining new attention in hurricane country.

356
00:28:30,860 --> 00:28:35,820
Unlike conventional structures, domes give wind very little surface space to latch onto.

357
00:28:35,821 --> 00:28:42,820
The shape of a concrete dome is absolutely perfect to withstand wind.

358
00:28:42,821 --> 00:28:46,820
There's only a very small area that the wind actually hits straight on.

359
00:28:46,821 --> 00:28:50,820
All the rest of it, it hits at an angle and flows around the dome itself.

360
00:28:50,821 --> 00:28:55,820
The only areas of the building that may be vulnerable to wind are the windows.

361
00:28:55,821 --> 00:29:02,820
When constructed in hurricane-prone areas, domes should be equipped with strong window lubers to seal them tight.

362
00:29:02,821 --> 00:29:09,820
Like the building itself, the construction of a dome requires simplicity, matched with ingenuity.

363
00:29:09,821 --> 00:29:14,820
The way we build a monolithic dome is we start off with an airform.

364
00:29:14,821 --> 00:29:16,820
It would remind you of a truck tarp.

365
00:29:16,821 --> 00:29:20,820
The fabric we use is actually quite stiff.

366
00:29:20,821 --> 00:29:24,820
It has stretch, but it's not like plastic or rubber bands.

367
00:29:24,821 --> 00:29:26,820
We have a computer-generated pattern.

368
00:29:26,821 --> 00:29:29,820
We put the pattern down on the fabric.

369
00:29:29,821 --> 00:29:31,820
We cut it out.

370
00:29:31,821 --> 00:29:38,300
It's welded together in the shape of the dome.

371
00:29:38,620 --> 00:29:40,920
We pour a concrete ring foundation.

372
00:29:41,420 --> 00:29:44,420
We attach the truck tarp to the foundation.

373
00:29:44,840 --> 00:29:48,460
We then take a fan, if you will, we call them inflators,

374
00:29:48,880 --> 00:29:51,880
and hook it to the dome and turn it on,

375
00:29:51,940 --> 00:29:53,460
and it blows up just like a balloon.

376
00:29:53,461 --> 00:29:57,660
And in larger buildings, we have two primarily for redundancy

377
00:29:57,661 --> 00:29:59,080
in case the power goes off.

378
00:29:59,081 --> 00:30:06,360
In the case of very large dorms, we may have four fans on it, but the majority of our dorms are built with one fan.

379
00:30:06,860 --> 00:30:12,520
Then we do all our work from inside, which is what's really unique about this kind of construction.

380
00:30:12,900 --> 00:30:17,140
We spray the inside surface with three inches of polyurethane foam.

381
00:30:17,620 --> 00:30:22,600
That's the world's best insulation, and it sets up in three to five seconds.

382
00:30:23,440 --> 00:30:26,620
Then we tie rebar to the underside of the foam.

383
00:30:26,621 --> 00:30:29,120
Rebar is reinforcing steel.

384
00:30:29,121 --> 00:30:34,120
It's usually bars of steel 3 eighths of an inch in diameter to a half inch.

385
00:30:34,121 --> 00:30:37,120
On big domes, it'll be up to an inch in diameter.

386
00:30:37,121 --> 00:30:42,120
And after the rebar is all tied in place, it looks like a birdcage inside.

387
00:30:42,121 --> 00:30:49,120
Then we spray concrete in again, up against the urethane, embedding all of that concrete.

388
00:30:49,121 --> 00:30:54,620
When that's done, we can turn the fans off and open the doors and windows.

389
00:30:54,621 --> 00:30:58,260
We now have one of the world's strongest structures.

390
00:30:59,400 --> 00:31:04,460
Dome buildings came under scrutiny after Hurricane Katrina slammed into the Louisiana Superdome.

391
00:31:06,120 --> 00:31:13,460
While it stood firm, it sustained damage because it wasn't constructed to withstand a hurricane greater than Category 3.

392
00:31:16,120 --> 00:31:22,660
Of all the structures built to withstand hurricane-force winds, perhaps the most carefully designed are skyscrapers.

393
00:31:22,661 --> 00:31:28,980
The skyscraper capital of the world, New York City, is vulnerable to hurricanes.

394
00:31:30,880 --> 00:31:37,600
On September 21st, 1938, one passed some 55 miles east of Manhattan.

395
00:31:38,680 --> 00:31:44,100
Had it made landfall on the island, many believe it would have caused extensive damage to the huge structures.

396
00:31:46,840 --> 00:31:51,060
With winds of more than 100 miles per hour taking aim at their massive walls,

397
00:31:51,920 --> 00:31:54,400
Giant buildings must be able to withstand the force.

398
00:31:54,900 --> 00:31:56,720
When designing today's high-rise buildings,

399
00:31:57,260 --> 00:31:59,820
architects and engineers use wind tunnels extensively

400
00:31:59,821 --> 00:32:02,900
before they commit a structure to concrete and steel.

401
00:32:03,900 --> 00:32:08,100
You really need to model the structure in a wind tunnel.

402
00:32:08,101 --> 00:32:11,380
Actually model a scale version and see how it behaves

403
00:32:11,381 --> 00:32:15,440
before you really understand the loads you have to deal with

404
00:32:15,441 --> 00:32:20,100
and the accelerations that occupants will feel during windstorms.

405
00:32:20,101 --> 00:32:27,800
Information collected from wind tunnel modeling has enabled designers to take a new approach to creating structures that can deflect wind more effectively.

406
00:32:29,140 --> 00:32:37,120
Moving beyond traditional square-cornered buildings, they can now design the corners of structures to have a more aerodynamic flow of wind around them.

407
00:32:39,400 --> 00:32:41,672
You can round the corners of a building.

408
00:32:41,673 --> 00:32:45,420
You can chamfer or 45-degree cut the corners of the building back.

409
00:32:45,421 --> 00:32:50,900
or you can create little stair steps, little sawtooth corners.

410
00:32:51,380 --> 00:32:53,800
New building designs can't come quickly enough

411
00:32:53,801 --> 00:32:59,120
because it seems the great storms are striking more frequently and ferociously than ever.

412
00:33:00,160 --> 00:33:04,100
Is global warming responsible for the recent super hurricanes?

413
00:33:06,520 --> 00:33:10,140
Pacific Ocean typhoons are often stronger than Atlantic hurricanes

414
00:33:10,141 --> 00:33:12,320
because they form over a larger ocean,

415
00:33:12,321 --> 00:33:15,620
which gives them more room to grow before they hit land.

416
00:33:17,080 --> 00:33:20,860
Nature Tech Hurricanes will return on Modern Marvels.

417
00:33:22,660 --> 00:33:27,240
We now return to Nature Tech Hurricanes on Modern Marvels.

418
00:33:29,120 --> 00:33:32,520
In 2005, the Hurricanes Katrina,

419
00:33:36,060 --> 00:33:36,760
Rita,

420
00:33:36,761 --> 00:33:45,460
and Wilma all struck within a 30-day period.

421
00:33:49,540 --> 00:33:51,840
Each reached Class 5 status,

422
00:33:52,400 --> 00:33:56,180
with wind speeds more than 155 miles per hour.

423
00:33:57,260 --> 00:34:00,460
Some refer to storms of this intensity as supercames.

424
00:34:00,461 --> 00:34:11,220
To many, it seems these giant storms are forming more often, and with greater strength than ever before.

425
00:34:12,840 --> 00:34:17,400
Some attribute this onslaught of hurricane activity to global warming.

426
00:34:19,300 --> 00:34:24,640
Each day, solar radiation hits the Earth's surface, warming the land and the sea.

427
00:34:24,641 --> 00:34:35,360
Some of this heat is radiated back into the atmosphere, where it's trapped by naturally occurring gases, like water vapor, methane, and carbon dioxide.

428
00:34:36,300 --> 00:34:40,020
This trapping of gases causes what is known as the greenhouse effect.

429
00:34:41,600 --> 00:34:47,920
For millions of years, like a good greenhouse, this system has kept the Earth's climate relatively stable.

430
00:34:49,440 --> 00:34:53,860
Yet during the last century, this global greenhouse has been warming up.

431
00:34:54,640 --> 00:35:00,640
Many believe that this is being caused by human activity or anthropogenic activity,

432
00:35:00,641 --> 00:35:06,640
specifically the burning of fossil fuels such as coal, gasoline, heating oil, natural gas.

433
00:35:06,641 --> 00:35:08,640
They produce carbon dioxide.

434
00:35:08,641 --> 00:35:14,640
We know for a fact that carbon dioxide levels in the atmosphere have been increasing over the last 100 years.

435
00:35:14,641 --> 00:35:20,640
And it's believed by many that this is causing the Earth to warm up.

436
00:35:20,641 --> 00:35:24,200
This change in the Earth's atmosphere may have an effect on hurricanes.

437
00:35:26,020 --> 00:35:31,420
The important thing for hurricanes is general warming of the globe.

438
00:35:32,300 --> 00:35:37,620
Pretty good evidence that the world's oceans in general are getting warmer.

439
00:35:38,460 --> 00:35:40,860
Warm oceans indeed affect hurricane conditions.

440
00:35:41,640 --> 00:35:45,480
But prudent scientists warn that the hurricane equation is much too complicated

441
00:35:45,481 --> 00:35:48,240
to be understood in terms of global warming alone.

442
00:35:48,241 --> 00:35:55,240
Weather records kept for decades reveal a frustrating inconsistency in storm trends.

443
00:35:56,420 --> 00:36:02,880
Yet despite this inconsistency, scientists now feel they've found a long-term pattern of hurricane activity.

444
00:36:03,680 --> 00:36:06,420
They call it interdecadal frequency.

445
00:36:09,120 --> 00:36:14,640
Interdecadal frequency refers to an oscillation or variation in the number of hurricanes.

446
00:36:14,641 --> 00:36:18,200
you'd find periods on the order of 10 or so years

447
00:36:18,201 --> 00:36:21,440
where you'd find a decreased number of hurricane occurrences

448
00:36:21,441 --> 00:36:24,480
and then over another period of 10 or more years

449
00:36:24,481 --> 00:36:25,920
you'd find an increase.

450
00:36:27,100 --> 00:36:30,320
Many hurricane forecasters believe that we are now in a period

451
00:36:30,321 --> 00:36:33,340
where we are in an upswing in the interdecadal frequencies

452
00:36:33,341 --> 00:36:37,360
and then presumably they believe that after a period of 10 or more years

453
00:36:37,361 --> 00:36:39,400
that the frequency would drop off again.

454
00:36:39,401 --> 00:36:49,000
Back in the 1930s up until 1969, we had a lot of hurricanes in the Atlantic, Caribbean, and Gulf of Mexico.

455
00:36:50,200 --> 00:36:56,060
From 1970 until 1995, there was a real lull.

456
00:36:57,080 --> 00:37:03,560
Beginning in 1995, we've had a lot of hurricanes.

457
00:37:04,560 --> 00:37:08,160
But this oscillation doesn't seem to depend on global warming.

458
00:37:09,400 --> 00:37:12,460
for complicated reasons involving ocean currents,

459
00:37:12,840 --> 00:37:18,000
the water of the Atlantic Ocean tends to get warmer and cooler over 20- or 30-year periods.

460
00:37:18,460 --> 00:37:20,820
These cycles seem to be completely natural.

461
00:37:21,300 --> 00:37:24,420
Now, global warming could be imposed on top of that,

462
00:37:24,540 --> 00:37:29,180
but the basic cycle seems to be just part of the way the world works.

463
00:37:29,780 --> 00:37:32,140
When you analyze this over the 50-year period,

464
00:37:32,141 --> 00:37:36,000
there's no clear indication that the number is either increasing or decreasing.

465
00:37:36,600 --> 00:37:39,340
In fact, it appears that it may be remaining the same.

466
00:37:39,400 --> 00:37:44,480
Although the number of hurricanes being produced may not be attributed to global warming,

467
00:37:45,040 --> 00:37:48,120
some believe their intensity has grown dramatically because of it.

468
00:37:49,080 --> 00:37:55,500
It's not definitive, but in principle, if global warming is causing increased sea surface temperatures,

469
00:37:55,960 --> 00:37:59,300
and there is some evidence that sea surface temperatures have increased,

470
00:38:00,000 --> 00:38:05,120
it is entirely possible that this would result in more severe hurricanes.

471
00:38:05,121 --> 00:38:13,100
The number of severe hurricanes, categories 3, 4, and 5, is double what it was about 15 or 20 years ago.

472
00:38:13,760 --> 00:38:15,840
And, you know, this raises some questions.

473
00:38:15,980 --> 00:38:21,040
On the other hand, when you listen to the people who've been forecasting hurricanes,

474
00:38:21,760 --> 00:38:26,080
they believe that this is just part of this interdecadal oscillation.

475
00:38:26,520 --> 00:38:32,540
And perhaps in past years there were a lot of severe hurricanes that we didn't know about because they didn't strike populated areas.

476
00:38:32,541 --> 00:38:37,540
So the verdict is still out on the effect of global warming on hurricanes.

477
00:38:37,541 --> 00:38:41,540
Is global warming causing increased sea surface temperatures which are producing this?

478
00:38:41,541 --> 00:38:46,540
Or is there some other phenomenon out there that's producing it that we don't understand yet?

479
00:38:46,541 --> 00:38:52,540
As time goes on, depending on the kind of correlations that we see, then we should know.

480
00:38:52,541 --> 00:38:58,540
Hurricane frequency and intensity are just two of the mysteries some scientists are exploring.

481
00:38:58,541 --> 00:39:02,540
Others are studying how to stop the deadly storms altogether.

482
00:39:02,541 --> 00:39:08,540
But is our current technology capable of stopping the most powerful storms on Earth?

483
00:39:08,541 --> 00:39:14,540
Ships have survived hurricanes by sailing fast enough to stay inside the eye.

484
00:39:14,541 --> 00:39:20,540
When this happens, flocks of seagulls often land on the ships and ride out the storm with them.

485
00:39:20,541 --> 00:39:25,540
Nature Tech – Hurricanes will return on Modern Marvels.

486
00:39:25,541 --> 00:39:30,340
We now return to Nature Tech, Hurricanes on Modern Marvels.

487
00:39:35,760 --> 00:39:38,840
Technology is helping us to better understand hurricanes.

488
00:39:39,620 --> 00:39:41,720
But can it answer the fundamental question?

489
00:39:42,200 --> 00:39:43,580
How do we stop them?

490
00:39:45,620 --> 00:39:50,760
In 1962, after several major hurricanes ravaged the East Coast,

491
00:39:50,940 --> 00:39:54,420
an ambitious project was launched called Storm Fury.

492
00:39:55,540 --> 00:40:01,460
The Weather Bureau, which became the National Weather Service, the Navy, various people

493
00:40:01,461 --> 00:40:02,460
were involved.

494
00:40:02,461 --> 00:40:06,980
Project Storm Fury from the early 1960s until 1982.

495
00:40:06,981 --> 00:40:12,260
And they had some hypotheses, some ideas of things you could do to weaken a hurricane.

496
00:40:12,261 --> 00:40:17,860
The project involved seeding clouds with ice-forming substances, like silver iodide, which would

497
00:40:17,861 --> 00:40:23,880
hopefully disrupt the storm's eyewall and cause it to collapse, thus reducing the winds.

498
00:40:23,881 --> 00:40:26,200
first implemented, the results were encouraging.

499
00:40:27,200 --> 00:40:33,340
The StormFury experimenters attacked four hurricanes on two occasions each, so a total

500
00:40:33,341 --> 00:40:34,260
of eight trials.

501
00:40:34,700 --> 00:40:39,080
Four of the eight tries to weaken a hurricane, the hurricane weakened.

502
00:40:39,840 --> 00:40:47,280
This was particularly true of Hurricane Debbie in 1969, where it weakened both times they

503
00:40:47,281 --> 00:40:50,960
hit it, and they thought they were making hurricanes weaken.

504
00:40:50,961 --> 00:40:57,400
But then, scientists learned as they studied hurricanes more, flew airplanes into hurricanes more and more,

505
00:40:57,520 --> 00:41:01,080
that storms naturally go through a cycle of growing stronger and weaker.

506
00:41:02,100 --> 00:41:09,460
And so nobody really knows that what the cloud seeding make Debbie get weaker, or was Debbie going to get weaker anyway.

507
00:41:09,860 --> 00:41:15,060
In addition to the inconclusive findings, the project faced some potential political problems,

508
00:41:15,380 --> 00:41:17,720
because it might be altering the natural course of the weather.

509
00:41:17,721 --> 00:41:30,743
The government said, you know, if we were to seed a hurricane and it were to trash Miami or Atlanta, Havana or any coastal city, regardless of what the seeding had

510
00:41:30,744 --> 00:41:33,060
done, we would look real bad.

511
00:41:33,061 --> 00:41:42,640
And essentially after 20 years of some really, really smart scientists trying to find a way to weaken a hurricane, not to make it go away,

512
00:41:43,120 --> 00:41:48,420
they decided that it just really can't be done reliably.

513
00:41:49,140 --> 00:41:51,800
But Project Storm Fury was far from a failure.

514
00:41:52,580 --> 00:41:57,240
Its contribution to hurricane science was pivotal in helping us understand the nature of the storms.

515
00:41:57,241 --> 00:42:04,380
In fact, the one thing that came out of the Storm Fury project, scientists learned a lot about hurricanes.

516
00:42:04,600 --> 00:42:07,320
They focused them. They had to really learn how hurricanes work.

517
00:42:08,480 --> 00:42:11,880
Hurricane scientists continue to propose other ways to weaken the storms.

518
00:42:12,600 --> 00:42:15,940
While they each have their merits, they also pose their own problems.

519
00:42:17,320 --> 00:42:22,720
One proposal involves towing icebergs into the path of a forming hurricane to lower the water temperature.

520
00:42:22,721 --> 00:42:27,720
There are a couple of problems, principally that it would take an awful lot of icebergs.

521
00:42:27,721 --> 00:42:32,720
Towing icebergs to put them in front of a particular hurricane isn't real practical

522
00:42:32,721 --> 00:42:36,720
because towing big objects across the ocean is really slow.

523
00:42:36,721 --> 00:42:42,720
If you tow an oil drilling platform, the tow moves at one or two nautical miles an hour,

524
00:42:42,721 --> 00:42:46,720
whereas a hurricane moves at 10 or 15 or 20 miles an hour.

525
00:42:46,721 --> 00:42:51,720
So getting the iceberg and the hurricane in the same place would be kind of tricky.

526
00:42:51,721 --> 00:42:55,280
Another proposal involves covering the ocean with a chemical film.

527
00:42:55,480 --> 00:42:57,820
There is one technique that would work.

528
00:42:58,420 --> 00:43:01,600
That's to spread a soap-like film on the ocean's surface

529
00:43:01,601 --> 00:43:06,460
that would slow down evaporation and deprive the hurricane of its energy.

530
00:43:06,940 --> 00:43:10,080
It would have to keep on working as the hurricane came through,

531
00:43:10,360 --> 00:43:12,820
and of course it would have to be environmentally benign.

532
00:43:13,240 --> 00:43:15,540
Nobody's found a substance that fits the bill.

533
00:43:16,100 --> 00:43:18,640
If somebody does, it would probably be worth trying.

534
00:43:18,641 --> 00:43:22,900
but so far we don't have any chemical that shows any promise.

535
00:43:23,140 --> 00:43:28,580
The substances that have been tried basically break up when the wind blows 10 or 15 miles an hour.

536
00:43:29,220 --> 00:43:32,040
So it seems for now Mother Nature has won the battle,

537
00:43:32,460 --> 00:43:35,020
and some wonder whether we should really be fighting her at all.

538
00:43:35,680 --> 00:43:40,040
Then there's a question, do you really want to try and control something like this?

539
00:43:40,041 --> 00:43:45,920
In other words, this is a natural occurrence that exists to take the buildup of energy near the equator,

540
00:43:45,921 --> 00:43:50,220
because that's where we get the most intense solar radiation flux

541
00:43:50,221 --> 00:43:53,140
and dissipate that energy toward the poles.

542
00:43:53,141 --> 00:44:00,140
So these hurricanes fill a needed purpose in maintaining our planetary energy budget.

543
00:44:00,500 --> 00:44:04,940
A much better way to spend your money is build buildings that stand up,

544
00:44:05,580 --> 00:44:10,460
figure out where you really should have them, improve evacuation, improve forecasting.

545
00:44:11,180 --> 00:44:15,160
But as for doing something to a hurricane, no.

546
00:44:15,920 --> 00:44:18,500
you're better off preparing for the hurricane.

547
00:44:19,960 --> 00:44:21,280
One thing is certain.

548
00:44:22,100 --> 00:44:23,680
Hurricanes aren't going away.

549
00:44:24,280 --> 00:44:26,880
It's only a matter of time before the next one hits.

550
00:44:27,720 --> 00:44:32,120
How prepared we are and how knowledgeable we are about this deadly force of nature

551
00:44:32,121 --> 00:44:36,800
will temper the toll it takes on our structures and our society.

552
00:44:41,180 --> 00:44:43,700
Have you seen the signs?

553
00:44:43,701 --> 00:44:45,800
Have you heard the news?

